      "description": "\"The sanctuary of the god Asclepius and his daughter Hygieia...was founded in the year 420/19 B.C. by an Athenian citizen from the deme of Acharnai, named Telemachos...The Temple of Asclepius is a building of the 1st century B.C., with a two-column in antis facade and a small cella, which according to Pausanias, who visited Athens in the 2nd century A.D., housed the statues of Asclepius and his children...The Doric Stoa...was built in 300/299 B.C. as epigraphical testimonies attest...The Ionic Stoa, is also dated to the last quarter of the 5th century B.C...In the 6th century A.D., when Christianity replaced paganism, all the buildings in the Asclepeion were integrated into the complex of a large three-aisled Early Christian basilica. In the Byzantine period (11th and 13th century) two smaller, single-aisle churches were erected on the site of the basilica.\" Information label on the site.",
